st: column 1 points (1,1)-(1,5); column 2 points (2,1)-(2,4). Are any of those points already covered? Possibly (2,5) not in T_5. So all points covered. So k=3 works.\n\nThus answer seems correct for n≥3.\n\nNow we need to verify that no other k values are possible. The solution's reasoning for impossibility of k=2 and k≥4 must be valid. Let's examine the reasoning.\n\nThe solution's Section 3: \"An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\". It argues:\n\n- Each sunny line meets any fixed column in at most one point (since otherwise vertical). That's correct: a non-vertical line can intersect a vertical line (column) at most once. Actually any line not vertical intersects a vertical line (x=c) at exactly one point (if not parallel). So a sunny line (non-vertical) meets each column at most one point. So each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines.\n\n- For any column, the remaining points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines are vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Horizontal and slope -1 lines also meet each column at most one point, while vertical lines meet a whole column. So to cover a column with more than k points, we need at least one vertical line among non-sunny lines.\n\n- Consider the three rightmost columns: sizes h_{n-2}=3, h_{n-1}=2, h_n=1.\n\n- If k ≤ 2 then column x=n-2 (size 3) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; thus one of the non-sunny lines must be vertical and must be the line x=n-2.\n\n- Then look at column x=n-1 (size 2). If we do not use a vertical line x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines, each sunny line must contain exactly one of them. The remaining column x=n (size 1) point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column n-1 because any line joining (n,1) with either point of column n-1 is either horizontal (slope 0) or slope -1, both non-sunny. Hence (n,1) would require a third sunny line, contradicting k ≤ 2. So k=2 impossible.\n\n- Then they argue k ≥ 4 also